  "query_preview": "..Are you enjoying the calm/dry weather? Better enjoy\nit while it lasts, because there is additional showers and\nthunderstorms forecast to return by Friday. Before we dive into\nthe upcoming unsettled weather, Arizona currently remain under a\nzonal flow, keeping afternoon highs near normal for this time of\nyear and winds light/variable. Dry air looks to stick around\nthrough much of Thursday, but increasing high clouds likely \nstarting tomorrow. \n\nThe initial push of moisture as early as Thursday night/Friday\nmorning, will be primarily from Tropical Storm Vance interacting\nwith a strong low pressure system forecast to push in from the\nPacific. Precipitation will be greatest along and south of the\nMogollon Rim, where rainfall amounts could range between 0.10-0.50\ninches. Elsewhere will stay drier with up to 0.10 inches possible.\nThis initial onset of moisture also looks to remain on the showery\nside, with only a few isolated thunderstorms expected. And\nthunderstorms that do form, will likely be from orographic\ninfluence. Saturday we could see a brief lull in activity as we \nwait for another shortwave to push in from off the southern \nCalifornia Coast. This second surge of moisture will be late \nSaturday through much of Sunday, where we could see more a more \ndynamic set-up, creating a more favorable environment for severe \nweather. There is still some uncertainty with the confidence of \nthe severe weather threat, so check back for more details as we \nget closer to the weekend. Outside of thunderstorm potential, \nthere is greater confidence for flooding concerns. \n\nIn short, dry/mild weather continue through Thursday, with\nshowers/thunderstorms likely returning Friday through the weekend.\nThe wet and potentially active weather could impact Halloween\nplans, so be sure to keep an eye on the forecast.",
